The Security Council,
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Recalling all its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question, in particular resolutions 2720 (2023), 2728 (2024) and 2735 (2024),
Welcoming the ceasefire agreement of 13 October 2025 and the release of living hostages and Palestinian detainees, and calling for the full and expeditious implementation of the agreement and the three-phase ceasefire proposal endorsed in resolution 2735 (2024),
Expressing grave concern at the loss of civilian life in Gaza and the West Bank, the dire humanitarian situation, and all violations of the ceasefire, including attacks against civilians and Israeli forces,
Condemning in the strongest terms the horrific terrorist attacks by Hamas on 7 October 2023, the taking and killing of hostages, conflict-related sexual violence, and the use of civilians and civilian infrastructure by Hamas and other terrorist groups,
Reaffirming the inherent right of all States to self-defence in accordance with the Charter and international law, and stressing the obligation of all parties to respect international humanitarian law and protect civilians,
Stressing the need for immediate, safe, sustained and unhindered humanitarian access to all civilians in Gaza, including through the opening of crossings and the restoration of essential services,
Rejecting any attempt at demographic or territorial change in the Gaza Strip, including any actions that reduce the territory of Gaza, and any forced displacement of civilians,
Reiterating its unwavering commitment to the two-State solution where Israel and Palestine live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, and stressing the importance of unifying the Gaza Strip with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority,
Recognizing the need for a credible political horizon, accountable governance, reconstruction and early recovery in Gaza, and for measures to prevent the rearmament and reconstitution of Hamas and other terrorist groups,
1. Welcomes the ceasefire agreement of 13 October 2025 and demands its full implementation without delay or condition, including a sustained ceasefire, the release of all remaining living hostages and the return of the remains of all deceased hostages, the release of Palestinian detainees consistent with the agreement, the withdrawal of Israeli forces as provided for in the agreement, and the return of Palestinian civilians to their homes and neighborhoods throughout Gaza;
2. Condemns in the strongest terms Hamas and other terrorist groups for their attacks on civilians, hostage-taking, sexual violence, use of human shields and use of civilian infrastructure for military purposes, and demands the immediate and unconditional release of all remaining living hostages and the return of the remains of all deceased hostages;
3. Demands that all parties fully respect the ceasefire, cease all acts of violence, exercise maximum restraint, refrain from reprisals and ensure the protection of civilians, humanitarian personnel, medical facilities and civilian infrastructure, in accordance with international humanitarian law;
4. Calls for immediate, safe, sustained and unhindered humanitarian access at scale to all persons in need throughout Gaza, including through all available crossings, and for the facilitation of fuel, food, water, medicine, shelter, electricity and essential services, as well as medical evacuations, and urges Member States to provide humanitarian funding in support of the United Nations and humanitarian partners;
5. Rejects any forced displacement, starvation, collective punishment or demographic or territorial change in Gaza, and stresses that all civilians must be allowed to return to their homes and neighborhoods in safety and dignity;
6. Stresses that Hamas must not be allowed to rearm, reconstitute or remain in control of Gaza, calls for the dismantling of terrorist infrastructure and the disarmament of terrorist groups, and calls upon all Member States to prevent the transfer of arms, financing and material support to Hamas and other terrorist groups;
7. Stresses the importance of a credible, irreversible and time-bound process toward the two-State solution, calls for Gaza to be unified with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority, and calls for Palestinian Authority reform, transparent governance, reconstruction and early recovery, with the support of the international community;
8. Calls on all parties to ensure accountability for violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including conflict-related sexual violence and violence against civilians in the West Bank, and calls for de-escalation in the West Bank, including East Jerusalem, and respect for the historic status quo of the holy sites;
9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 30 days, and thereafter every 60 days, on the implementation of this resolution, including on the release of all hostages and remains, humanitarian access, the delivery of assistance and progress on governance and reconstruction in Gaza;
10. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

